germany's foreign minister says he wants  to do everything legally possible to  help iraqi kurds fight the militants  whether that includes sending german  weapons though looks unlikely for the  moment the defense minister says other  forms of aid are the main priority  we're looking to see what help we can  provide  it could be in the form of blankets  tents and medical supplies  but also helmets bulletproof vests night  vision equipment and trucks to name a  few items  but with the un warning of more  atrocities and even a potential genocide  some voices say that's not enough  elmar brock from the european parliament  is in favor of sending weapons directly  to the kurdish authorities  i think they have better state  structures in place than the current  rulers in baghdad who are engaged in  political infighting  so i think we'd make better progress  tackling these problems by dealing  directly with the kurds  others fear those weapons could fall  into the wrong hands and warned that  simply sending more arms into iraq might  not solve the country's problems  in addition to the questions of offering  military and humanitarian aid we also  have to think about what to do with all  of the weapons from the country's past  from what we've seen there are already  too many weapons in iraq  germany wants to coordinate its aid  efforts with its european partners  it will have the chance to do that at a  special summit of eu foreign ministers  on friday
 

